===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ʃɪˈbæŋ(ɡ)/}}
* {{rhymes|en|æŋ|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
{{unk|en}}. {{etydate|1862|nodot=1}} with the meaning “temporary shelter”. The modern sense of “matter of concern” is from 1869; “vehicle” is from 1871–2.<ref name="tow">[http://www.takeourword.com/TOW193/page2.html Take our Word]</ref><ref name="wo">{{R:Wordorigins.org|whole_shebang_the|whole shebang, the|Tuesday, February 20, 2007}}</ref>

In the sense of “temporary shelter”, it was perhaps brought by US Civil War Confederate enlistees from Louisiana, from {{uder|en|fr|chabane||hut, cabin}}, a dialectal form of {{uder|en|fr|cabane||a covered hut, lodge, cabin}} (see ''[[cabin]]'', ''[[cabana]]''). Alternatively, that sense may be from or have been influenced by {{m|en|shebeen||cabin where unlicensed liquor is sold and drunk}}, attested pre-1800, chiefly in Ireland and Scotland, from {{uder|en|ga|síbín||illicit whiskey}}, a diminutive of {{m|ga|síob||a drift}}.

The vehicle sense is perhaps from the unrelated {{uder|en|fr|char-à-banc||bus-like wagon with many seats}}. The sense of “matter of concern” is hypothetically from either, or onomatopoeia.

(The term was ''not'', as is sometimes claimed, commonly used by prisoners at {{w|Andersonville National Historic Site|Andersonville}} in reference to their shelters. [//www.nps.gov/ande/historyculture/myth-shebang.htm According to the US National Park Service], "While shebang was a term sometimes used to describe prisoner shelters at Andersonville, its usage was probably quite limited. In some 1,200 pages of postwar testimony by prisoners held at Andersonville, the word appears four times, and is virtually absent from most prisoner diaries and contemporary memoirs." The terms ''burrow'', ''dugout'', ''hut'', ''lean-to'', ''shanty'', ''shelter'' and ''tent'' are far more common.)

