====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|informal|US|archaic}} A [[lean-to]] or temporary [[shelter]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1862|author={{w|Walt Whitman}}|title=Journal|month=December
|passage=Their '''shebang''' enclosures of bushes.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1889|author=[[w:Bret Harte|Bret Harte]]|title=[[s:The Heritage of Dedlow Marsh|The Heritage of Dedlow Marsh]]
|passage=They say that old pirate, Kingfisher Culpepper, had a stock of the real thing from Robertson County laid in his '''shebang''' on the Marsh just before he died.}}
# {{lb|en|informal|US|archaic}} A place or building; a [[store]], [[saloon]], or [[brothel]].
# {{lb|en|informal}} Any matter of present concern; thing; or business; most commonly in the phrase "the [[whole shebang]]".
#: {{syn|en|jimbang}}
#* '''1869''', {{w|Samuel Clemens}} ({{w|Mark Twain}}), letter to publisher:<ref name="wo" />
#*: I like the book, I like you and your style and your business vim, and believe the '''chebang''' will be a success.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1934|author={{w|Robert E. Howard}}|title=[[s:Sluggers on the Beach|Sluggers on the Beach]]
|passage="Before I'd share anything with you," he said bitterly, "I’d lose the whole '''shebang'''."}}
# {{lb|en|informal|obsolete}} A [[vehicle]].<ref>''Take our Word''</ref>
#* '''1871''', December 14, {{w|Samuel Clemens}} ({{w|Mark Twain}}), “Roughing It” (lecture), printed in Fred W. Lorch, “Mark Twain’s Lecture from Roughing it”, in ''American Literature'', volume 22, number 3 (November 1950), pages 305:
#*: <!-- [sic] all in one paragraph --> {{...}} So they got into the empty omnibus and sat down. Colonel Jack says: “...What is the name of this.”<!-- [sic] period, not question mark --> Colonel Jim told him it was a [[barouche]]. After a while he poked his head out in front and said to the driver, “I say, Johnny, this suits ''me''. We want this '''shebang''' all day. Let the horses go.”

